ear would have the same chance of holding a daily record. Since 2000, Lynchburg has set <strong>87 standing record lows</strong>, close to the 73 that chance alone would give, and <strong>68 record highs</strong>, close to the 73 that chance alone would give. The 1930s hold the most record highs of any decade (73). Lynchburg's all-time high is 106\u00b0F, on July 10, 1936.","t_chg":"Lynchburg's nights cooled over the long run. Its days have warmed since 1986.","p_chg":"Across 132 complete years: Lynchburg's average low has fallen <strong class='sig'>\u22120.11\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range \u22120.17 to \u22120.06), about \u22121.5\u00b0F since 1893. Lynchburg's average high shows no clear change: +0.02\u00b0F per decade, a range (\u22120.04 to +0.09) that includes zero. <strong>The last 40 years tell a different story:</strong> since 1986, lows show no clear change and highs have risen <strong class='sig'>+0.71\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.34 to +1.07). The full record joins different observing sites over the years, and a move (for example, from the city to a cooler airport) can hide or exaggerate a long-run trend, so the recent decades are the clearer guide here.","t_mon":"Lows rose in none; 4 months fell. Highs rose in 2 of 12 months; May fell.","t_rain":"Rain and snow: no clear long-term change","p_rain":"Lynchburg's yearly precipitation shows no clear long-term change (+0.27 inches per decade, a range that includes zero). The 1991\u20132020 normal is 42.7 inches a year. The wettest year was 2020 (69.73 in); the driest, 1930 (19.83 in).","show_snow":true,"sig_bars":{"lo":false,"hi":false},"t_year":"2026 so far: 13 daily record highs, 3 daily record lows"}}